::ENG3_U04_L05_GP_Q01::[MLA.ENG3.W.02] Passage: A locked gate holds roses just beyond the bars. Source note: Gates can symbolize boundaries, while flowers can suggest desire or growth. Which claim best supports source-supported literary analysis? {
~The gate is old and the roses are flowers.#This only identifies details and does not make an analytical claim.
=The locked gate and roses symbolize desire that remains visible but unreachable.#Correct. This claim interprets literary elements and can be supported with source information.
~The roses are probably difficult to reach because gates are hard.#This is literal and informal.
~The source note is about gates and flowers.#This repeats the source instead of applying it.
}
::ENG3_U04_L05_GP_Q02::[MLA.ENG3.R.01] Passage: The lighthouse beam crosses empty water and flashes against houses where no sailors live anymore. Which evidence best supports analysis of light imagery as memory? {
~The lighthouse is near water.#This detail is setting, but it is not the strongest evidence for memory.
~The harbor is closed.#This supports abandonment, but not the light imagery directly.
=The beam still flashes against houses where sailors no longer live.#Correct. This detail connects the light image to memory of absent people.
~The lamp turns each night.#This is relevant but less complete than the answer connecting light to absent sailors.
}
::ENG3_U04_L05_GP_Q03::[MLA.ENG3.R.09] Passage: The narrator says, The city wore its windows like tired eyes. Source note: Personification gives human qualities to nonhuman things. Which analysis best explains the figurative language? {
~The city has many windows, and people have eyes.#This notices comparison but does not explain meaning.
~The sentence proves the city is alive.#This misreads figurative language literally.
~The narrator dislikes windows.#This invents an unsupported attitude.
=The personification makes the city seem exhausted, giving the setting an emotional quality.#Correct. This explains the figurative language and its effect.
}
::ENG3_U04_L05_GP_Q04::[MLA.ENG3.W.04] Draft: The lighthouse is about memory and the source says light can mean memory or hope and it is important. Which revision improves clarity and style? {
~The lighthouse is about memory, and also the source says light can mean memory or hope, and it matters.#This remains repetitive and vague.
~The lighthouse means stuff because of light and source support.#This is informal and unclear.
=The lighthouse beam suggests memory because it continues to shine on houses connected to sailors who are gone, and the source explains that light imagery can signal memory or hope.#Correct. This revision is clearer, more specific, and better structured.
~Light is a thing in the lighthouse passage and sources say things about it.#This is too vague.
}
::ENG3_U04_L05_GP_Q05::[MLA.ENG3.R.09] Passage: A traveler enters a valley where every road becomes a mirror. Source note: Allegory often uses a literal journey to represent an abstract struggle. Which analysis best explains possible allegory? {
=The mirrored roads may create an allegory for self-examination because the journey forces the traveler to face reflections at every choice.#Correct. This explains both literal journey and symbolic meaning.
~The traveler sees mirrors because the roads are shiny.#This stays literal and misses allegorical meaning.
~The valley is confusing because roads should not be mirrors.#This is reaction, not analysis.
~The source note explains allegory, but the passage is about roads.#This separates connected ideas.
}
